## 14k vs. 18k Rose Gold for a Radiant Cut Ring

This is the most counterintuitive fact about rose gold: 14k is MORE pink than 18k.

Rose gold gets its blush color from copper. The more copper in the alloy, the more vivid the pink tone. 14k rose gold is 58.3% pure gold — the remaining 41.7% is primarily copper. 18k rose gold is 75% pure gold — which means less copper and a softer, more champagne-toned blush.

If you want the most vivid, distinctly pink rose gold, 14k is the correct choice. This surprises buyers who assume higher karat = better. In rose gold, higher karat means less pink, not more.

**14k rose gold** is harder, more scratch-resistant, more vividly pink, and less expensive than 18k. All settings in the tables above are 14k. The extra hardness in 14k protects the prongs that grip the radiant cut's clipped corners, which matter for long-term stone security.

**18k rose gold** is softer, develops fine scratches faster, costs 20–40% more, and produces a subtler champagne-blush tone rather than the vivid pink of 14k. The savings from choosing 14k over 18k are better directed toward the center stone.

The right choice for daily-wear is 14k. It costs less, looks more pink, and wears better. There is no tradeoff.

